 "notes": "I read lines 3349–3437 with Read and checked every row against the text.\n\nHow many rows and marks: there are 53 stations, numbered 115–167 with no gaps, which agrees with the lead-in's \"fifty-three\". But 39 rows carry *, not the \"thirty-four\" the lead-in gives. Either the stated count or the marks are wrong in the print, and nothing on the page says which. The asterisks are counted separately as 3 Isle of Man, 4 N. W. England, 7 North Wales, 6 South Wales, 6 Bristol Channel and 13 South-Western England. 130 St Tudwal's has keepers named (\"Messrs Davies and Parsons\") but no *. It is also the only named row ending its station name in a full stop (\"St Tudwal's.\") rather than a comma. 144 Usk has * but no keepers.\n\nHow I recorded things:\n- Fragments drop only each line's leading indentation. Spacing inside the line is kept exactly, including the column padding.\n- Names keep their trailing punctuation as printed: a comma before keepers, a full stop otherwise. For the clean place name, strip it.\n- Keepers keep their title as printed (Mr / Mr. / Messrs). I dropped the full stop that ends each row. Each \"Messrs …\" group is one entry, following your Black and Beggs example.\n- Two rows wrap onto a second line, and in both I joined the keepers with a single space: 121 Selker gives \"Messrs Daniel Kneale and George Cumming\" and 126 Skerries Rock gives \"Messrs Rees, Smith, Tomkinson, and Williams\".\n- Coast is the sub-heading the row sits under. No row prints a county or place of its own, so place is null throughout. The list gives no light characters, so character is null throughout.\n\nCopied as printed but worth checking:\n- 128 has \"Mr. W. R. Burgess\", the only \"Mr.\" with a full stop.\n- 122 has \"Henry Clavell\" with no Mr.\n- 140 is \"Nash (E. )\", with a space before the bracket.\n- These spellings are as printed: 118 \"Chickens Rock\" (usually Chicken Rock), 124 \"Air\" (Point of Air), 137 \"Caldy\" (Caldey), 154 \"Sevenstones\", 142 \"Flatholm\".\n- 139 Scarweather has \"Messrs Havells and Wyness\". \"Havells\" may be a misprint for Howells, the name at 138 Helwick, but I kept it as printed.\n- 161 has \"Ebgar\", which is odd but also kept.\n\nCross-check: the thanks paragraph that follows (lines 3428–3436) names Ainger and Gaudion, Bowen, Clark, Knott, Nicholas (Nash), Norton, Rees and Smith (Skerries), Steer, Thomas and Hughes, Walters and Johnson, and Webber and Howells. All of these match the list, and every one of their stations is marked *.",
